#---
# purpose: "a C `int` result is its low 32 bits read as signed, so a negative one stays negative on every door"
#---
#
# # c_int_negative_return
#
# `open`, `close` and `abs` all return C `int`, which is 32 bits wide. A C
# function returning one writes only the low half of the integer return
# register and says nothing about the upper half. x86-64 glibc leaves that
# half zero, so reading the whole register turns `open`'s -1 error sentinel
# into 4294967295. arm64 libcs happen to write a full 64-bit -1, which is why
# the same program answered correctly on one machine and wrongly on another.
#
# The width belongs to the declaration, not to the reader: `c_int` says the
# symbol returns a C `int`, and each door sign-extends the half register it
# actually got. This fixture pins both signs, because sign extension of a
# non-negative value is indistinguishable from reading the whole register.
#
# Both call shapes are here. `open` is declared variadic, so its result comes
# back through the generated variadic call; `close` and `abs` are fixed-arity
# and come back through the ordinary extern call. A `c_int` parameter is the
# same fact on the way in: `close(-1)` hands over the low 32 bits of -1 and
# the kernel reports EBADF rather than being handed a 64-bit value it has no
# way to read.
extern "C" defp libc::open(path :: c_string, flags :: c_int, ...) :: c_int
extern "C" defp libc::close(fd :: c_int) :: c_int
extern "C" defp libc::abs(c_int) :: c_int

def main() do
  dbg(libc::open("/fz_c_int_negative_return_missing", 0, 0o644 :: integer))
  dbg(libc::close(-1))
  dbg(libc::abs(-7))
end
